var WK_KOREA = [
{"id":"korea-bulgogi","name":"Bulgogi","nameAlt":"Marinated Beef — the word means 'fire meat'","aliases":["bulgogi","korean bbq beef","sobulgogi"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","grill","bbq","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","bbq"],"ingredients":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain · 20ml soy sauce · 12ml pear or apple juice (the tenderiser that is not a secret) · 10ml mirin or dry sherry · 10ml sesame oil · 5g brown sugar · 8g garlic, minced · 3g ginger, grated · 2 spring onions, chopped · a pinch of black pepper","method":"THIN SLICES ARE THE DISH. Bulgogi means 'fire meat' and its entire character is built on slices so thin they cook in seconds on a screaming-hot pan — about 3mm, cut across the grain. Part-freeze the beef for 30 minutes and it slices like butter. NO SHEET OF BEEF SHOULD BE THICKER THAN A TOASTED SANDWICH.\n\nTHE MARINADE IS THE TWIST AND THE PEAR IS THE TRICK. Whisk the soy, pear juice, mirin, sesame oil, brown sugar, garlic, ginger and pepper. The pear is doing real chemistry: its enzymes (and apple's, its nearest shelf cousin) tenderise the meat — and its sweetness caramelises into that lacquered, savoury-sweet edge. This is the difference between Korean barbecue and plain grilled meat.\n\nMARINATE 30 MINUTES TO OVERNIGHT. Thirty minutes is the floor, overnight is the peak — the pear's enzymes need time to work their way in, and the soy deepens. Do not salt the beef first: the marinade is the seasoning.\n\nSEAR FAST, IN TWO BATCHES, IN A SMOKING PAN. Get a pan or griddle ripping hot and sear the slices in a single layer, 30–45 seconds a side, until the edges catch and caramelise. Crowd the pan and the beef steams grey — space the slices and it lacquers. Two batches are always better than one crowded one.\n\nSERVE STRAIGHT OFF THE PAN, SCATTERED WITH THE RESERVED SPRING ONION. Bulgogi waits for no one — the contrast of hot caramelised edges against cool rice and sharp kimchi is the whole plate. Leftover marinade, boiled 3 minutes in a pan, is the classic dipping sauce.","cookTime":"Prep 15–20 min · Marinate 30 min–overnight · Cook 3–4 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 36g · Carbs 16g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Marinated raw beef keeps 2 days in the fridge. Cooked bulgogi keeps 3 days and is superb cold — and famously folds into tomorrow's fried rice. Freezes well raw, in the marinade, for a month.","chefNotes":"The pear is not optional decoration — its enzymes do the tenderising that makes bulgogi silk instead of steak. Slice thin, marinate long, and sear in batches over a roaring pan. The caramelised, lacquered edges are the point.","pairsWith":"Steamed white rice · Kimchi · A bowl of sliced cucumber in vinegar · Cold beer","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bulgogi's name literally means 'fire meat' — and it has been cooked over open flame since the days of the Goguryeo kingdom, around 2,000 years ago. The sweet lacquered soy marinade is the modern chapter: it only became the bulgogi the world knows in the 20th century, when the sesame-oil-and-pear glaze perfected what the fire had started.","howThisFeels":"The hiss as the thin slices hit a screaming-hot pan, the buttery-sweet smoke rising, and then the first caramelised bite against cold rice — the whole table suddenly very quiet.","leftovers":["Chop leftover bulgogi small and fold it into hot rice with a spoon of gochujang and sesame — bulgogi fried rice, the family's favourite day-two use.","Wrap cold bulgogi with lettuce and a smear of gochujang for ssam — a crisp, spicy hand roll that might be the best version of all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken Bulgogi (Dak Bulgogi)","icon":"🐔","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same pear-and-soy lacquer on chicken thigh — juicier than breast, and it takes the glaze even better.","trivia":"Dak bulgogi uses the same marinade with chicken thighs — and thighs are the right call: the extra fat keeps the fast-seared slices silky where breast would dry out in the same 45 seconds.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g chicken thighs, sliced thin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Chicken must cook through, so give it a minute per side rather than 45 seconds, and let it rest one minute before serving. The pear marinade works exactly the same — the enzymes don't know what kind of meat they're tenderising."}]}},{"name":"Pork Bulgogi (Dweji Bulgogi)","icon":"🐖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same lacquer on pork — richer, and the most common street-version of Korean BBQ outside Korea itself.","trivia":"Dweji bulgogi swaps in pork shoulder, and it is the bulgogi you are most likely to meet from a Korean street stall — pork is cheaper than beef, the marinade loves it, and the caramelisation is even faster.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g pork shoulder, sliced thin against the grain"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Pork shoulder wants a touch more time than beef — sear a full 90 seconds per side so it's cooked through — and skip the resting hang-up: pork is short-shrift if you prefer it well done."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-bibimbap","name":"Bibimbap","nameAlt":"The 'mixed rice' bowl — every colour of the Korean table in one bowl","aliases":["bibimbap","korean mixed rice","dolsot bibimbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","bowl","vegetables","egg"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox"],"ingredients":"80g cooked rice · 40g beef mince (optional) · 1 egg · 30g spinach · 30g carrot (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g courgette (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g bean sprouts · 15g sesame oil · 10ml soy sauce · 5g gochujang (the red paste that makes it bibimbap) · 1 clove garlic · a pinch of sugar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"BIBIMBAP MEANS 'MIXED RICE' AND THE MIX IS THE MEAL. Line up the vegetables, cook each in its own oil, and pile them around the rice in the national colours: green spinach, orange carrot, pale sprouts, white rice, and the red gochujang on top. The bowl should look like a small garden before it is stirred.\n\n⚖️ EVERY VEGETABLE IS SEASONED, NOT JUST COOKED. Each one gets its own minute in the pan with a whisper of salt and, ideally, a drop of sesame oil: spinach wilted and seasoned with soy and garlic; carrot and courgette stir-fried separately so their colours and textures stay distinct. Seasoned separately is the difference between bibimbap and a rice bowl with vegetables on it.\n\nTHE EGG IS THE LAST ACT — FROM THE PAN, STRAIGHT TO THE TOP. Fry it sunny-side up with a soft, runny yolk — the yolk is the sauce of the second half of this dish, the one that binds the mix when you stir at the table.\n\nIF YOU HAVE BEEF, BROWN IT HARD FIRST. Season the mince and cook it in a hot pan until properly brown — then reserve a spoon of its pan juice to stir into the finished bowl.\n\nNOW THE MOVEMENT THE DISH IS NAMED FOR: STIR. The runny yolk, the gochujang, the sesame oil and every separate flavour are folded into the rice with chopsticks until every grain is glossy and red — bibimbap is a dish you complete at the table, not on the stove. Scatter toasted sesame seeds over the stirred bowl.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Carbs 68g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best prepared and stirred fresh. The cooked rice and vegetables keep separately 2 days in the fridge — the vegetables re-warm well in a pan; the rice in the microwave. The dish does not freeze well (the vegetables weep on thawing).","chefNotes":"Season each vegetable in its own pan minute — that is the whole textural argument of the bowl. Get real gochujang (Korean chilli paste) if you can find it; it is the red heart of the mix. And never stir it on the stove: the stirring IS the ritual, done at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of soup (clear or kimchi) on the side · Cold barley or green tea","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bibimbap exists because of Korean jesa — the ancestor memorial rites that once laid out many small dishes in a specific order. Leftovers and small portions were mixed into rice so nothing was wasted, and the 'mixed rice' became so beloved it shed its leftovers-only history entirely. In Jeonju, the dish's spiritual home, the version is famously luxurious, topped with raw beef and egg yolk.","howThisFeels":"A bowl of colour that looks almost too pretty to stir — then the runny yolk, the gochujang and the sesame oil are folded in and it becomes the happiest thing in the kitchen.","leftovers":["The next-day move is kimchi fried rice — chop any leftover vegetables and the beef into a hot pan with the cold rice and a spoon of gochujang, and fry it until it crackles.","Leftover rice and vegetables make a perfect lunchbox bibimbap — keep the gochujang in its own little tub and stir it in at the table."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Dolsot (Sizzling Stone Bowl)","icon":"🔥","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The restaurant version with a soundtrack — served in a screaming-hot stone bowl that crackles when the rice touches it, and leaves a crust of crispy golden rice at the bottom.","trivia":"Dolsot bibimbap arrives in a stone bowl heated until smoking — the rice that touches the hot stone fries into a crispy golden crust (nurungji) that is half the pleasure. You can fake most of the experience with a cast-iron pan or a screaming-hot ovenproof bowl: heat the dish and a spoon of sesame oil in it, then build the bibimbap inside.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"sesame oil, for the hot bowl"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Heat the stone bowl or a small cast-iron pan over high heat with a teaspoon of sesame oil until it shimmers. Build the rice and toppings directly in the hot bowl — the rice will crackle and crisp on the bottom as it cooks. Serve sizzling, with the warning that the bowl is dangerously hot."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"Every colour of the bibimbap garden, no meat — with mushrooms stepping in for the beef's savour.","trivia":"A vegetarian bibimbap is never a thin version in Korea: the dish is fundamentally a mixture of seasoned vegetables, and a good shiitake or shimeji mushroom, browned hard, closes most of the gap the mince would leave.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef mince (optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g mushrooms, sliced and browned"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the sliced mushrooms hard in a hot dry pan — no oil for the first minute so they release water — then add a drop of soy and sesame at the end. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. For a fully vegan bowl, finish with a spoon of crispy fried tofu in place of the egg."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-fried-rice","name":"Kimchi Fried Rice","nameAlt":"Kimchi Bokkeumbap — Korea's favourite fridge-clearing pan","aliases":["kimchi bokkeumbap","kimchi fried rice","bokkeumbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","stir-fry","quick","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","quick","lunchbox","bachelor"],"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional, for extra heat) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced · a splash of vegetable oil","method":"COLD RICE, NOT FRESH. Day-old rice from the fridge fries loose and separate; fresh rice is swollen with steam and turns to porridge in the pan. No day-old rice? Spread fresh rice on a tray and freeze it 20 minutes to dry it out. THE COLD RICE IS THE WHOLE FIRST RULE.\n\nFRY THE KIMCHI HARD FIRST — THIS IS THE DISH'S SECRET SOUL. Chop the kimchi, squeeze out its juice and keep the juice, then fry the chopped kimchi in a hot pan with the oil until the edges catch and it darkens — usually 3 minutes. Raw kimchi tastes raw; fried kimchi turns sweet, sour and deeply savoury. That 3 minutes of browning is the entire difference between kimchi fried rice and rice with kimchi stirred in.\n\nADD THE GOCHUJANG AND THE RICE AND SMASH THE SAUCE INTO IT. Push the fried kimchi to one side, drop in the gochujang and let it sizzle in the oil, then the rice, and press everything flat with the spatula — let the rice toast against the hot pan for 30 seconds before you toss it, so the grains catch golden. WHICH IS ALSO THE WOK HEI OF KIMCHI FRIED RICE.\n\nSEASON AND TOSS. Splash in the soy, the sesame oil, the sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice — the juice recreates the fermented tang that frying was cooking off. Toss until every grain is glossy and red.\n\nCROWN WITH A FRIED EGG. Sunny-side-up, runny yolk — the yolk is the second sauce of the dish, broken at the table and stirred into the hot rice. Scatter spring onion and sesame seeds on top.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 66g · Fat 17g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best the minute it's fried. Keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats without shame in a hot pan with a splash of water. Freezes acceptably for a month — though the egg never does (add it fresh).","chefNotes":"Fry the chopped kimchi until the edges catch — that browning is the whole move. Use day-old rice, let it toast in the pan for 30 seconds before tossing, and never skip the fried egg on top: the runny yolk is half the sauce.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang soup · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Bulgogi","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi fried rice was born as the ultimate fridge-clearing dish — the last-of-the-pantry move Korean students and bachelors perfected. It got so beloved that dedicated kimchi fried rice restaurants exist across Korea, serving bowls with a matching fried egg as the default, and it is regularly voted Korean comfort food.","howThisFeels":"The sharp fermented smell of kimchi hitting a screaming-hot pan, the edges catching and darkening — and then a red, glossy bowl of rice with a fried egg winking at you from the top.","leftovers":["Fold a slice of cheese into the hot rice with the lid on and let it melt — cheese kimchi fried rice is a legitimate Korean favourite, salty-tangy and unholy-good.","Wrap it in a sheet of nori with the fried egg and a little gochujang for a kimbap-style hand roll the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The pantry hero — old rice, older kimchi, one egg, and the whole pan smells like Seoul.","trivia":"The classic is the fridge-clearing original: whatever the crisper had is welcome, but the kimchi and the fried egg are the law. In Korea this is the bachelor's dish, the student's dish, and the midnight snack that outranks takeaway.","delta":{},"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced","method":["Fry the chopped kimchi hard until its edges darken, 3 minutes.","Push aside, sizzle the gochujang in the oil, add the cold rice and let it toast 30 seconds before tossing.","Season with soy, sesame oil, sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice.","Crown with the fried egg and spring onion. Stir the yolk into the rice at the table."]},{"name":"Bacon & Cheese","icon":"🥓","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The Western-Korean cross that works — smoky bacon and a molten cheese crust over the tangy rice, a heart attack you will happily eat twice.","trivia":"Bacon and cheese are the two great Western additions to kimchi fried rice — cheese because its fat cuts the sharp kimchi acid beautifully, bacon because its smoke plays against the ferment like it was born to. Both are now standard-issue in Seoul's fried-rice bars.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g bacon, chopped"},{"item":"20g mozzarella or cheddar, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the chopped bacon first, crisp it, and use its rendered fat in place of the oil for the kimchi — the kimchi fries in bacon fat, which is exactly as good as it sounds. After the rice is fried and seasoned, press it flat, scatter the cheese over, cover the pan for 1 minute until it melts into a molten crust, and eat straight from the pan."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-tteokbokki","name":"Tteokbokki","nameAlt":"The beloved street-food rice cakes in a spicy-sweet red sauce","aliases":["tteokbokki","spicy rice cakes","topokki"],"course":"main","type":["main","street-food","rice-cake","quick"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","quick","snack"],"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes (tteok – fresh or frozen) · 20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang (Korean chilli paste) · 8g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or mild red chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or brown sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":"THE RICE CAKES ARE THE WHOLE TEXTURE. Tteokbokki is all about glossy, chewy cylinders of rice cake — cut them if frozen, and if they are fresh and soft, no soaking needed. If frozen, soak in cold water 10 minutes first so they heat through without going rubbery. THEY SHOULD BE SO CHEWY THEY ALMOST SQUEAK ON YOUR TEETH.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS THE SOUL AND IT IS BUILT IN THE PAN. Combine the water (or anchovy-kelp stock for depth), gochujang, gochugaru, soy sauce and honey in the pan — this is a sauce that comes together in the pan, not in a bowl first, because the simmering is what thickens and blends it.\n\n⚖️ THE TRUE STOCK IS THE SECRET. Anchovy-kelp stock (dashima stock) is what the street vendors use, and it is the difference between tteokbokki and noodles in a chilli packet. A 5cm piece of dried kelp simmered in the water for 5 minutes, then removed, is enough to change the whole bowl — the shop shortcut is a splash of good broth or a scrap of kombu.\n\nBRING TO A BUBBLE, THEN ADD THE RICE CAKES. Simmer over a medium flame, stirring gently, for 8–10 minutes — the sauce reduces, thickens and clings to the cakes. Gently is the word: rice cakes are fragile and sticky, and a hard stir breaks them into mush. Add the fish cakes and boiled egg for the last 3 minutes, and the spring onion off the heat.\n\nSERVE INSTANTLY, WITH A SPOONFUL OF THE THICK SAUCE OVER EVERYTHING. The chewy rice cakes carry the sweet-spicy sauce like nothing else — and the classic finish is to mop the last sauce with a piece of fried (or even plain) gimbap.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 12 min","kcal":"~430 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 80g · Fat 6g · Sodium ~950mg","storage":"Best eaten immediately — rice cakes harden and turn off as they cool. Keeps a day in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water to loosen the sauce. Does not freeze (the texture is everything).","chefNotes":"Chewy rice cakes and a glossy sweet-spicy sauce are the whole dish — simmer gently so the cakes don't crack, and let the sauce reduce until it clings like a glaze. If you can find real gochugaru, the flakes give the proper fiery red; if not, mild chilli flakes plus a little extra gochujang is honest. The anchovy-kelp stock is the street-vendor secret — worth the two extra minutes.","pairsWith":"Fried or plain gimbap (rice rolls) to mop the sauce · A boiled egg plopped in the sauce · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Tteokbokki started life in a palace kitchen as a royal dish — sliced rice cakes simmered in a soy-meat broth, the 'palace tteokbokki'. After the Korean War, a street vendor swapped the soy for the soldier-delivered gochujang that was suddenly everywhere, and the fiery red street version was born. The street version won: today the red tteokbokki is the dish, and it is one of Korea's best-selling street foods, served from the same vendor stalls in winter.","howThisFeels":"Glossy red cylinders of rice cake — the first bite is the purest sweet-spicy-chewy hit the Korean street has to offer, with the sauce sticking to your fingers and asking for more.","leftovers":["The classic Korean finish: keep the leftover red sauce and fry gimbap, or rice, in it — sauce-too-good-to-waste is part of the street-food code.","Add the leftover sauce to a pan of instant noodles for a cheat tteokbokki-ramen hybrid the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Street","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The original street bowl — chewy rice cakes in a glossy red sauce, with fish cake and a boiled egg as the classic companions.","trivia":"The street version includes paper-thin fish cakes by default — they flavour the sauce as they simmer and become the chew that balances the rice cake's chew. The boiled egg is the optional regular: halved into the sauce, it soaks up the red and becomes the best bite in the bowl.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes · 20g fish cakes (optional) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang · 8g gochugaru or mild chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":["Soak frozen rice cakes in cold water 10 minutes.","Simmer the stock (or water plus a scrap of kombu, removed after 5 minutes) in a pan.","Whisk in gochujang, gochugaru, soy and honey; bubble to a glossy red sauce.","Add the rice cakes and simmer gently 8–10 min until the sauce clings; add fish cake and egg for the last 3 min.","Spring onion off the heat; serve immediately."]},{"name":"Cheese Tteokbokki","icon":"🧀","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The modern favourite — molten mozzarella over the fiery red cakes, the fat calming the fire like a benediction.","trivia":"Cheese tteokbokki is the 21st-century Seoul standard — the volcanic-red sauce meets a cooling blanket of melted cheese and the combination became so popular it now rivals the original at street stalls.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g mozzarella, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Once the sauce has thickened and clings to the cakes, scatter the grated mozzarella over the top, cover the pan 1–2 minutes until it melts into a molten blanket, and eat with the cheese pulling off every cake."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-japchae","name":"Japchae","nameAlt":"The glass-noodle stir-fry that is a party's sweetest moment","aliases":["japchae","korean glass noodles","chap chae"],"course":"side","type":["side","noodles","banquet","vegetables","make-ahead"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","party","banquet","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles (dangmyeon – the round, slippery ones) · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot (fine matchsticks) · 30g onion (thinly sliced) · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms (soaked, sliced) · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":"THE NOODLES ARE SWEET-POTATO GLASS NOODLES AND THEY ARE THE WHOLE POINT. Japchae's signature is dangmyeon — translucent, glassy, gloriously slippery sweet-potato noodles. Cook them in boiling water 6–8 minutes until just tender, drain, and CUT them with scissors a few times so they are a manageable length — they are spectacularly springy. Do not rinse them; the starch coats them and helps the sauce cling.\n\n⚖️ EVERY COMPONENT IS COOKED SEPARATELY, THEN MIXED — THE HALLMARK OF A BANQUET DISH. This is not a one-pan stir-fry: the carrot, onion, mushrooms and spinach each take their own oil and their own minute, and each is seasoned at its turn. The separate cooking is what lets every colour and texture survive the final mix. HOLDING THE COMPONENTS APART IS THE WHOLE METHOD.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS A SWEET-SALTY GLOSS, MADE ALL AT ONCE. Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, minced garlic and a pinch of pepper — sweet forward, salty behind, the classic banchan balance. Have it in a cup before the pan goes on.\n\nSEAR THE BEEF FIRST IN ITS OWN MINUTE. Season it lightly, sear it hard and brief in a hot pan until just cooked — it should be pink to medium, never grey.\n\nNOW THE FINAL TOSS: ADD THE NOODLES, THE VEGETABLES, THE BEEF, THE SAUCE — TOSS ONCE, COVERED, AND LET EVERYTHING DRINK. Toss over low heat for 2 minutes so the sauce edges into everything, and finish with sesame seeds and a final thread of sesame oil. Serve warm or chilled — this is one of the great make-ahead dishes of Korean cooking and is better on day two.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 12g · Carbs 68g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Japchae is a legendary make-ahead — it keeps 3–4 days in the fridge and is genuinely better on day two as the sauce soaks in. Serve it cold or at room temperature, which is how it is traditionally offered at banquets. Freezes well for a month. Do not reheat hard — the noodles are perfect cool.","chefNotes":"Sweet potato glass noodles are non-negotiable. Cook every vegetable in its own pan with its own seasoning, keep them apart until the very end, then toss them all once in the sweet-salty sesame gloss. The separate cooking is what makes japchae japchae. Sesame oil, sesame seeds, soy and a spoon of sugar — the entire Korean binding spell.","pairsWith":"Korean barbecue (the classic banquet pairing) · Pickles · Cold noodles or soup on the side","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Japchae was born at the royal court in the early 1600s, when a court official served the king a dish of stir-fried vegetables and glass noodles so good it named itself: japchae means 'mixed vegetables'. It became a banquet standard and later went fully royal — King Gojong is said to have raised it to banquet status in the 19th century. Every celebration table in Korea still sets out a bowl.","howThisFeels":"Long, slippery, glassy noodles glistening with sesame oil and studded with bright vegetables — a banquet plate that somehow tastes better the next day.","leftovers":["The day-two move is the unsung one: serve it chilled, straight from the fridge — that is how Korean banquets offer it, and the cold noodles drink the sauce even deeper.","Fold leftover japchae into an omelette or a rice bowl with a fried egg for a five-minute Korean lunch."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beef","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"The banquet original — thin-sliced beef and bright vegetables through the glossy glass noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the royal-era version: thinly sliced beef, shiitake, carrot, spinach and onion, each cooked on its own and finished in the sweet sesame glaze. It is one of the most festive things you can put on a Korean table.","delta":{},"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":["Cook the glass noodles 6–8 min until tender; drain and cut with scissors.","Cook each vegetable separately in its own sesame-oiled pan, seasoning as you go.","Sear the beef hard and brief until pink, never grey.","Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, garlic and pepper.","Toss everything once with the sauce over low heat; finish with sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Mushroom (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"All the glassy-slick glory with mushrooms doing the beef's savoury work — the vegan banquet version.","trivia":"Simply removing the beef and browning an extra variety of mushrooms — shiitake, oyster or button — covers the savour beautifully, and a shot of soy in the mushroom pan deepens the whole plate. Japanese and Korean temple kitchens have been making an egg-free, beef-free japchae for centuries.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g extra mushrooms (oyster or button), sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the extra mushrooms hard in a dry hot pan until they give up their water and turn golden, then add a splash of soy and a drop of sesame to finish them. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. The mushrooms take the sweet-salty glaze beautifully and the plate loses nothing."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-samgyeopsal","name":"Samgyeopsal","nameAlt":"Grilled pork belly — the social dish Korean nights are built around","aliases":["samgyeopsal","grilled pork belly","korean bbq pork belly","ssam"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","grill","bbq","social","make-your-own"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","social","casual","groups"],"mood":"Social & festive","time":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","health":"Rich and indulgent — balance with raw greens and garlic, which are part of the ritual, not an afterthought.","allergen":"Soy (ssamjang, soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil in the ssamjang)","budget":"★☆☆ — belly is the treat; the lettuce-garlic-chilli table is cheap","audience":"Perfect for a weekend group — it is a do-it-yourself grill where nobody needs a plate, just lettuce and a lap.","season":"Best year-round; a summer braai where the grill IS the party and rain just moves it indoors","ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang (or mix 20g gochujang with 15g doenjang and a drop of honey) · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla (kkaennip, if you can find it) · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · freshly ground black pepper","method":"SAMGYEOPSAL IS NOT A RECIPE, IT IS A RITUAL — AND THE RITUAL IS THE TABLE. The meat arrives sizzling in the middle, and everyone cooks, wraps and eats at their own pace. The first rule is REFUSAL: samgyeopsal is NEVER marinated. The whole point is clean pork fat — the seasoning happens at the table in the wrap, which is why this is the one Korean meat that goes un-doctored onto the grill. The second rule is the grill itself. Lay the thick slices on a hot grill or heavy pan, cut-side down, and let them wallow in their own fat. Turn only when the underside is deeply golden and the edges are crisp — patient char beats constant fidgeting. As each piece cooks, cut it with scissors into bite-size strips right on the grill, letting the cut edges crisp another minute. This scissors-at-the-table move is not theatre; it keeps the juices inside and gives every tongue-sized piece a crunchy edge.\n\nWHILE IT COOKS, BUILD THE SSAM TABLE — this is half the dish. Down the centre goes a bowl of ssamjang (shop-bought is honestly fine; homemade is a 2-minute mix of gochujang, doenjang, a thread of sesame oil and a crush of garlic). Around it: cool lettuce leaves (or perilla if you can find it — its anise-and-mint edge with pork fat is one of the great Korean tastes), raw garlic sliced paper-thin, green chilli rings, and a little dish of seasoned sesame oil — sesame oil with a pinch of salt and black pepper, for a quick dip before the wrap.\n\nNOW THE WRAP THAT NAMES THE DISH: SSAM. Take a leaf, swipe a dab of ssamjang in the middle, lay in a piece of the hot pork with its crisp edge showing, a slice of raw garlic, a ring of chilli, fold the leaf over it like a little parcel and eat it whole in one mouthful. Do not nibble — a ssam is built for a single satisfying bite, and the cold leaf against the hot fat, the raw garlic, the chilli and the salty paste colliding at once is exactly why Koreans eat this nightly.\n\nA bowl of steamed rice is optional but honest: for the last few pieces, grill the cloves of leftover garlic alongside the pork until they are soft and sweet, and tuck them into the final wrap. Wash down with cold soju or beer, and repeat until the grill is empty.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","kcal":"~550 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 6g · Fat 46g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best eaten at the grill, fresh from the pan. Cooked leftover belly keeps 2 days in the fridge — the fat sets and it is superb cold in rice or sliced into fried rice. Do not reheat hard; the belly turns from crisp to wobbly.","chefNotes":"Never marinate it — samgyeopsal is pure pork, and the table is the seasoning. Char it patiently, cut it at the table, and never skimp the garlic and lettuce: they are not garnishes, they are the balance that makes a fat-soaked meal feel light.","pairsWith":"Cold soju or crisp lager · Kimchi · Doenjang-jjigae on the side · More lettuce — always more lettuce","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Doenjang-jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Samgyeopsal means 'three-layered flesh' — and the name is about the cut, not the cooking. Pork belly is one of the most popular foods in Korea precisely because it is unmarinated: while almost every other meat arrives drenched in soy or gochujang, this one is served raw at the table, and the seasoning happens in the lettuce wrap. Korean barbecue restaurants built entirely around samgyeopsal number in the thousands, and the 'samgyeopsal + soju' combo is so iconic it has its own slang name: sam-sam, from the similar syllables. The ritual of one person cooking for the table, cutting the meat with scissors and tucking it into leaves for friends first, is so deeply social that Koreans debate whether it tastes better shared — and the honest answer is yes.","howThisFeels":"The sizzle of unmarinated belly curling at the edges, the bright sting of raw garlic and chilli against cool lettuce, and the whole table reaching for the ssamjang at once — nobody talks much during samgyeopsal, and everyone is happy.","leftovers":["Chop leftover belly small and fry it with kimchi, cold rice and a spoon of gochujang for samgyeopsal kimchi fried rice — the classic next-day move.","Slice it thin and crown a bowl of steaming rice with a rare soft egg for one of Korea's favourite solo comfort lunches.","Use the leftover ssamjang as a rapid stir-fry sauce — a tablespoon with any chop of vegetables becomes an instant Korean bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Grill (Samgyeopsal)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":60,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — thick belly, patient char, scissors at the table, and the ssam table dressed with raw garlic and chilli.","trivia":"The classic is the complete experience: the unmarinated belly, the ssamjang-and-garlic table, and the lettuce-wrap rule of one full bite per ssam.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · black pepper","method":["Grill the unmarinated belly slices cut-side down, turning only when deeply golden; cut into strips with scissors at the table.","Set the ssam table: ssamjang, lettuce or perilla, raw garlic, chilli rings, and seasoned sesame oil.","Press each piece into the salt, swipe with ssamjang, and wrap in a leaf with garlic and chilli — one whole bite per wrap.","Repeat until the grill is empty, washing down with cold soju or beer."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Pan-Fried Belly Strips","icon":"💰","costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The same unmarinated pork, but sliced thin and pan-fried fast instead of grilled — the apartment weeknight version that still gets the ssam table.","trivia":"Slicing the belly thin (about 5mm) is the budget-quick trick: it cooks in two minutes a side in a hot pan with no grill required, and because it is thinner it needs less total fat rendered. The ssam table does the rest of the work exactly as the classic.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g pork belly, in thick slices","to":"200g pork belly, pre-sliced thin (about 5mm)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A thin slice needs only a minute or two per side in a hot, dry pan. Let the first side go a full 90 seconds untouched so it develops a proper golden crust, then flip, stack the slices into a little pile, and serve immediately with the ssam table. No scissors needed — they are already bite-sized."}]}},{"name":"Grilled Tofu & Mushroom Ssam (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same ssam ritual, with the grill given over to tofu and mushrooms — and honestly, this could be the best version: charred tofu soaks up the ssamjang and the fun of the wrap is unchanged.","trivia":"Korean temple kitchens have always eaten this way — grilling firm tofu until it holds a golden crust and wrapping it in lettuce with ssamjang. The tofu does what the belly cannot: it drinks the paste rather than shedding fat, and the sesame-oil seasoning that replaces pork fat brings a nutty depth of its own.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g pork belly, in thick slices"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g firm tofu, cut into thick slabs"},{"item":"100g shiitake or other mushrooms, thick-sliced"},{"item":"extra sesame oil for brushing"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu slabs dry, brush both faces with sesame oil, and grill until deeply golden on both sides — a proper crust, not a pale surface. Brush the mushrooms with the same oil and grill until they release their water and take colour. Build the wraps exactly as the classic: ssamjang, garlic, chilli, leaf. For a fully vegan bowl, make ssamjang from gochujang and doenjang and a drop of maple instead of honey."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-sundubu-jjigae","name":"Sundubu-jjigae","nameAlt":"Soft tofu stew — the bubbling volcano pot Koreans eat on cold nights","aliases":["sundubu","soft tofu stew","sundubu jjigae","tofu stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","tofu","spicy","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","lunch"],"mood":"Comforting & restorative","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","health":"High-protein and light — soft tofu is genuinely good for you, and this is one of the lightest comfort stews in Korean cooking.","allergen":"Soy (tofu, soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang in some versions) · seafood in the classic (optional — remove for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — tofu and pantry staples; a little pork or seafood stretches the classic, or skip them entirely for the cheapest honest bowl","audience":"Perfect for anyone who needs a gentle, warming bowl — it is Korea's fever-and-cold-night food for good reason.","season":"Winter and rainy days are its home, but Koreans crave it year-round","ingredients":"1 packet soft/silken tofu (about 300g) · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock (or vegetable stock for vegan) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or 1 tbsp mild chilli flakes + a pinch more heat · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1 egg (optional — classic) · 40g pork belly or 50g prawns (optional — classic) · black pepper","method":"THE STEW LIVES IN A STONE POT, AND THE POT IS HALF THE DISH. The classic is cooked in a ttukbaegi — a small earthenware pot that sits on the stove bubbling, and arrives still bubbling at the table, the tofu quivering like it is alive. No ttukbaegi? A small heavy saucepan does the same job; the drama is just quieter.\n\nFIRST, THE STOCK — THIS IS WHERE THE DEPTH COMES FROM. True sundubu is built on anchovy-kelp stock (see the Dashi card for the technique family), and it is what separates this from a box of tofu in chilli water. Simmer the big kelp scrap in the stock base with dried anchovies for 5 minutes, then remove them. For a vegan pot, use vegetable stock with a strip of dried shiitake soaked in for the same savoury depth.\n\n⚖️ THE SPICE LAYER IS BUILT IN THE PAN, NOT DUMPED IN. Heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the garlic and onion, and fry gently for a minute; then the gochugaru and let it bloom in the oil for 30 raw seconds — this is the step that turns flat chilli powder into a rich, red, fragrant base. Stir in the gochujang and soy until the paste dissolves into the oil.\n\nNOW THE STOCK AND THE TOFU. Pour in the stock and bring to a bubble. Add the protein if using — pork belly slices or prawns — and simmer 5 minutes. Then the starring act: spoon the soft tofu in big, impossibly fragile pieces, letting them float rather than stirring them into nothing. The tofu is never cut with a knife and never chopped in a bowl; it is scooped in whole and breaks apart naturally as the stew bubbles — that irregular, patchy texture is the soul of sundubu.\n\n⚖️ THE EGG GOES IN AT THE TABLE, NOT THE STOVE. Crack it into the bubbling centre, turn the heat off, and let the residual bubbling cook the white while the yolk stays molten. The yolk is the finishing sauce of the stew, broken with a spoon right before eating. Serve immediately, straight from the pot, with a bowl of steamed rice — the Korean rule is to alternate bite of stew, bite off plain rice, because the rice is the palette that makes the heat sing, not the thing to be drowned in it.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 24g · Carbs 18g · Fat 24g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Sundubu is at its best the moment it is made — the soft tofu is at its silkiest straight from the bubbling pot. Leftover stew keeps a day in the fridge, but the tofu weeps and firms; reheat gently and add a fresh spoon of stock to re-liquefy. The egg is always fresh, cracked at the table.","chefNotes":"Three rules make or break sundubu: a proper kelp-anchovy stock (the depth is in the broth, not the chilli), the spice bloomed in oil before the liquid goes in, and the tofu scooped in whole and never stirred. The egg is the finishing act, always at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of plain steamed rice · Kimchi · The same leaves of seasoned seaweed · Cold barley tea between spoonfuls","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Korean Fried Chicken","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Sundubu-jjigae means 'soft tofu stew', and the word 'also' hides real history: sundubu is a Korean-style tofu that is older and firmer than Japanese silken tofu, made the traditional way with natural seawater as the coagulant. The stew became iconic in the 1980s when Korean immigrant grandmothers opened 'sundubu' restaurants in Los Angeles, and the bubbling stone-pot bowl quickly became a Korean-American institution — today it is one of the most-ordered items in Korean food, famous for arriving at the table still boiling, with the raw egg in the diner's control.","howThisFeels":"The whole pot arriving at the table still bubbling, the soft tofu wobbling like a live thing in red broth, and that first warm spoonful of stew followed by a cooling bite of plain rice — pure comfort in liquid form.","leftovers":["Spoon the leftover stew over cold rice with a sesame-oil thread for a warm-cold jjigae donburi — the absorbed broth is the point.","Freeze the stew base (before the tofu and egg) in a tub; a fresh scoop of soft tofu and 5 minutes rebuilds the whole pot.","Drain the stew solids and stir them into an omelette with a little extra sesame — the captured umami disappears into the eggs."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork & Soft Tofu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The classic balance — a few slices of pork belly give the broth a gentle richness that the soft tofu then cuts right through.","trivia":"Pork and tofu are a classic Korean pairing — the meat adds a little fat and a little sweetness to the otherwise clean, fiery broth, and the soft tofu swallows it all. This is the everyday home version, not the seafood restaurant one.","delta":{},"ingredients":"1 packet soft tofu · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 30g onion · 1 spring onion · 1 egg · 40g pork belly, sliced thin · black pepper","method":["Simmer kelp and anchovies in the stock base 5 minutes; strain.","Bloom the garlic and gochugaru in sesame oil, then the gochujang and soy.","Add the stock, then the pork belly; simmer 5 minutes.","Spoon in the tofu in big fragile pieces — never stir it.","Bubble 3 minutes, crack the egg in at the table, serve with rice."]},{"name":"Budget — Egg & Kimchi Only (Vegan-friendly)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"No pork, no seafood — just good stock, bloomed chilli and the star of the show, soft tofu. The cheapest honest bowl and genuinely the nearest to the soul of the dish.","trivia":"The temple version of sundubu is exactly this — no meat, no fish broth, just depth from kelp and shiitake, and this is how many Koreans actually eat it at home. The egg is optional; skip it for a fully vegan pot.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g pork belly, sliced thin"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g kimchi, chopped"},{"item":"extra shiitake for the stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the chopped kimchi with the stock for its fermented tang and a deeper colour. Use a vegetable stock properly deepened with dried shiitake and kelp — the umami carries the whole bowl with no meat at all. Skip the egg for the fully vegan version, or keep it for the classic yolk-as-sauce finish."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Sundubu","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Bottled anchovy stock and a supermarket packet of soft tofu — the quickest honest sundubu, still bubbling, still with the egg at the table.","trivia":"Even in Korea, the weekday shortcut is a good bottled stock and a packet of soft tofu from the convenience store. The depth that matters is the spice bloom and the never-stir tofu rule — those are the techniques, not the homemade stock.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a quality bottled anchovy-kelp stock (or instant dashi stock powder, the legitimate Korean shortcut) in place of the homemade simmer. Everything else identical — the bloom, the tofu spooned whole, the egg at the table. The whole pot comes together in about 15 minutes."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-fried-chicken","name":"Yangnyeom Korean Fried Chicken","nameAlt":"Double-fried chicken in a glossy gochujang glaze — chimaek's other half","aliases":["yangnyeom chicken","korean fried chicken","kfc korean","double fried chicken","chimaek"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","party","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","comfort","sports","everyday"],"mood":"High-energy & celebratory","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min (including the double fry)","health":"Fried — this is an indulgence, but the double fry actually renders MORE fat out of the skin, leaving it crisper AND slightly leaner than a single fry.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · gluten (flour in the coating)","budget":"★★☆ — chicken pieces are cheap; the sauce is pantry staples","audience":"The party platter — made for sharing with cold beer (chimaek) and fingers.","season":"Game night and celebration food, year-round — but the craving strikes hardest with cold beer in hand","ingredients":"300g chicken pieces (wings or thighs) · 40g cornflour + 20g plain flour for the coating · 1 egg (optional, for a thicker crust) · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru or 1 tbsp sweet chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce (the Korean fusion nod) · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · toasted sesame seeds · oil for frying","method":"THE DOUBLE FRY IS NOT A TECHNIQUE, IT IS THE RECIPE — and it is the entire reason Korean fried chicken eats like a different animal from any other fried chicken. The first fry at a lower heat cooks the chicken through and pushes moisture and fat out; a 5-minute rest; then a SECOND fry at high heat in the same oil, which turns the already-set crust glassy and shatter-crisp. That second crunch is what survives the glaze — a single-fried chicken goes soggy the instant the sauce touches it; a double-fried one stays crackly for an hour. The cornflour in the coating is the second secret: cornflour has no gluten, so it fries into a light, crisp shell instead of a chewy wheat one.\n\nBEFORE YOU FRY, THE COATING. Season the chicken and toss it in a dry mix of 2 parts cornflour to 1 part plain flour. The dry-coat matters: a dry, powdery coat is what blisters into that airy crackle. Rest the coated pieces 10 minutes so the coating sets and grips.\n\nTHE FIRST FRY: 160–165°C, about 7 minutes, until pale gold and just cooked through. The oil should be at a steady gentle bubble — too hot and the outside scorches before the middle cooks. Lift out onto a rack (never paper towel, which steams the bottom), and rest 5 minutes.\n\nTHE SECOND FRY: crank the oil to 185–190°C and fry again 3–4 minutes until deep golden and rock-hard crisp. Drain on the rack again.\n\n⚖️ THE GLAZE IS A PAN, NOT A BOIL. In a separate pan over medium heat, soften the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame oil and garlic together for a minute until glossy and warm — the gochujang is the soul: a fermented chilli paste whose slow umami and gentle heat is why this tastes deep, not just spicy-sweet. Toss the hot chicken through the warm glaze just enough to coat, then out. (For the absolute crispiest version, brush the glaze on instead of tossing — this is the wing-specialist's move.)\n\nFINISH with sesame seeds and sliced spring onion, and eat immediately with cold beer — the pairing is so sacred in Korea it has its own name: chimaek (chi from chicken, maek from beer).","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~600 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 48g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best straight from the fryer while the crunch sings. Keeps 2 days in the fridge — but re-crisp in a hot oven for 5 minutes, never the microwave, which ruins the glassy crust on contact. The glaze, refrigerated separately, keeps a week and vanishes over noodles.","chefNotes":"Double-fry or don't bother — the second fry is the entire dish. Dry-coat with cornflour for a light glassy shell. Glaze from a pan, not a boil. And brush the glaze if you want maximum crunch; toss it if you want maximum clove-on-garlic coating.","pairsWith":"Cold beer (non-negotiable — it IS chimaek) · Pickled radish (the classic yellow cubes, or a sharp local pickle) · More cold beer","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Korean fried chicken's rise is one of the great fried-food stories. American soldiers brought Southern fried chicken to Korea after the Korean War, and Korean cooks immediately saw the improvement: add a double fry and a sticky-sweet-spicy glaze, and the dish became its own creature — Korea's most-loved delivery food, with fried-chicken restaurants numbering in the tens of thousands. The drink pairing (chimaek) is so culturally wired that marriage-hungry singles in Korea are said to have tried 'chimaek blind dates' — first dates built entirely on chicken and beer. And the glaze's secret ingredient is often a whisper of tomato sauce: not a Western intrusion but a Korean fusion that has been standard since the 1980s, balancing the gochujang's savour with a touch of sweetness.","howThisFeels":"The impossible crunch of the second fry, then the glossy red glaze hitting hot chicken — the smell of garlic and gochujang filling the kitchen, and someone already twisting the cap off a cold beer.","leftovers":["Chop cold leftover pieces small and toss them through rice with the glaze and sesame for a 5-minute yangnyeom fried-rice bowl.","Cold chicken, pulled off the bone, is the star of a kimchi fried-rice with a fried egg the next morning.","The leftover glaze is a sauce for anything — noodles, tofu, or just a spoon (nobody is judging at home)."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Yangnyeom (Gochujang Glaze)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The full double-fry with the glossy sweet-spicy-gochujang glaze — the chimaek centrepiece.","trivia":"The classic is the 1980s Seoul formulation — double-fried wings in a gochujang-honey-garlic-tomato glaze. It is the dish that made Korean fried chicken famous worldwide.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g chicken pieces · 40g cornflour · 20g plain flour · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce · 2 cloves garlic · spring onion · sesame seeds · oil","method":["Dry-coat seasoned chicken in cornflour-flour mix; rest 10 minutes.","First fry at 160°C, 7 minutes — pale gold, just cooked; rest on a rack 5 minutes.","Second fry at 190°C, 3-4 minutes — deep gold and rock-crisp.","Glaze in a warm pan: gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame, garlic.","Toss hot chicken through the warm glaze; finish with sesame and spring onion. Serve with cold beer — it is chimaek."]},{"name":"Budget — Single-Fry Soy-Garlic Sticky","icon":"💰","costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"No double fry, a simpler soy-garlic-sugar glaze — still properly crisped and sticky, just without the glass-shatter of the full method.","trivia":"The budget fork trades the second fry for a longer single fry at a steady medium heat, which gets most of the crunch — and swaps the gochujang glaze for a classic soy-garlic-sugar sticky sauce, which is arguably the more traditional Korean flavour (the gochujang glaze is the newer star).","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the coated chicken in a single batch at a steady 175°C for 10-12 minutes until deep golden and fully cooked, then drain on a rack. For the glaze, simmer soy, brown sugar and crushed garlic to a shine — a spoon of gochujang optional. Toss and serve. Most of the crunch, all of the flavour, about half the oil temperature juggling."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Crispy Oven Wings with Yangnyeom","icon":"⚡","costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The weeknight recipe — dry-baked wings got properly crisp in a hot oven, then the same glossy gochujang glaze.","trivia":"A sprinkle of baking powder over the dry-coated wings, baked at a high heat, crisps them surprisingly close to a fry — the baking powder breaks down into tiny bubbles on the skin. It is the classic Korean-American home shortcut.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Toss the coated wings with ½ tsp baking powder, spread on a rack over a tray, and bake at 220°C for 40 minutes, turning once, until deeply golden and crisp. Warm the glaze, coat, and serve — the oven does the double-fry's job from the outside in."}]}},{"name":"Sticky Crispy Tofu (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The impossible-crunch tofu version — the same glossy gochujang glaze, and tofu that cracks like it belongs in the double-fry tray.","trivia":"Firm tofu pressed, coated in cornflour and double-fried gives a glassy shell that genuinely holds the glaze — the Korean vegan-fried-chicken answer, and it is better than it has any right to be.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"300g chicken pieces"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g firm tofu, pressed and cut into thick slabs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu 20 minutes, cut into slabs, coat in cornflour and double-fry exactly as the classic. The cornflour crust is what gives the crackle. For a fully vegan glaze, swap the honey for brown sugar — everything else is naturally vegan (gochujang, soy, tomato sauce)."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-jjigae","name":"Kimchi Jjigae","nameAlt":"Kimchi stew — the fridge song that uses up OLD kimchi","aliases":["kimchi jjigae","kimchi stew","doenjang kimchi stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","kimchi","one-pot","comfort","lunch"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","winter","comfort","leftover"],"mood":"Warm, tangy & soulful","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","health":"Fermented and probiotic — the older kimchi used here is richest in lactobacilli, and this is one of Korea's most comforting fermented meals.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang, tofu) · seafood in the classic tuna version (swap for 'tofu-only' for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — one of the cheapest comfort stews there is, built on the kimchi already in the fridge","audience":"The everyday home stew — a bowl of kimchi jjigae is Korea's answer to a grilled-cheese-and-soup night.","season":"Winter is its throne — the colder the day, the better the jjigae — but it rescues nothing-but-old-kimchi nights year-round","ingredients":"100g OLD kimchi (the older, the deeper — days to weeks old is ideal) · 1 knob pork belly or a small tin of tuna (optional, the richness) · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · a pinch of sugar · black pepper","method":"THE STEW IS A FRIDGE-CLEARING SONG, WRITTEN AROUND OLD KIMCHI — and the older the kimchi, the better the jjigae. Fresh kimchi snaps and fizzes like a jar salad; ageing kimchi turns deeply sour, effervescent and mellow, and it is that aged sourness that carries a whole stew. If your kimchi is young and bright, the honest fix is a splash of vinegar and a pinch of sugar to fake the fermentation curve, and let it cook longer.\n\n⚖️ THE KIMCHI IS SEARED BEFORE IT MEETS ANY LIQUID. This is the step nobody skips in a proper Korean kitchen: heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the drained, roughly chopped kimchi, and fry it HARD until its edges char and the whole pan smells intensely of fermented chilli — about 3 minutes. Raw kimchi dropped into broth tastes like soup with kimchi in it; seared kimchi flavours the entire stew from within. Save the kimchi juice too — a spoon of it goes in with the liquid for extra tang.\n\nNOW THE PROTEIN. Press the pork belly slices (or drained tuna) into the seared kimchi and let them brown with it — this is the 1-minute step that loans the whole pot its deep, savoury backbone. If you are keeping it vegan, skip straight to the broth.\n\nIN WITH THE LIQUID AND THE SAVOURY PASTE. Add the water (or stock), the gochujang, soy, garlic and that pinch of sugar (the sugar bridges the ferment's sourness and the gochujang's funk). Simmer 15 minutes until the kimchi is soft and the broth is red and glossy. The stew can be thin — jjigae is brothier than a curry — but it should coat the spoon.\n\n⚖️ THE TOFU GOES IN LAST, AND STAYS A QUIET FOOTNOTE. Cube firm tofu — never soft, in this stew — add it in the last 5 minutes, and let the heat soak it without stirring it to breaking. Serve bubbling hot with a bowl of steamed rice beside, and alternate bites: stew, then plain rice, then stew. The plain rice is not a side in Korea; it is the cooling palate that lets the heat keep singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 18g · Carbs 16g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"Kimchi jjigae is famously better on day two — the kimchi keeps fermenting and the flavour rounds. Keeps 4 days in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water. Freezes well in portions for a month. The tofu is best added fresh each time.","chefNotes":"The three rules: USE OLD KIMCHI (the sourness is the heart), SEAR THE KIMCHI hard before the liquid goes in (this is where the flavour builds), and ADD THE TOFU LAST so it stays intact. A spoon of kimchi juice in the broth is the classic extra tang.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · More kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang-jjigae or a quick side of cucumber banchan","goesWith":["Sundubu Jjigae","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi jjigae is the true kitchen-sink stew of Korea — the opposite of the fresh, careful banchan. It exists entirely as a use for OLD kimchi: the jars that have been fermenting in the fridge for days and weeks, too sour and mellow to eat raw as banchan, are exactly right for this pot. Every Korean household has a version, and the family argument is usually about pork vs tuna — the pork version is richer, and a small tin of tuna (a beloved budget addition) brings a mellow fishiness that pairs shockingly well with the sour kimchi. In fact, the tuna version is so common in Korean kitchens that tinned tuna and kimchi are considered a match made in the pantry. In Korea the stew is often the winter comfort food that marks the return of cold weather — the first kimchi-jjigae night of autumn is practically a seasonal ritual.","howThisFeels":"The smell of kimchi searing hard in sesame oil before the liquid even goes in — then a bubbling red pot on the table, the tofu bobbing, and the first spoonful that is somehow both sour and comforting all the way down.","leftovers":["The day-two jjigae is a matter of pride — reheat gently, add a splash of water, and the fermented broth is even deeper.","Spoon it over cold rice and top with an egg for a lazy jjigae donburi.","Stir a spoonful into fried rice or noodles for an instant flavour bomb."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Belly Kimchi Jjigae","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seared kimchi, browned pork slices, and a long simmer that turns the broth red, glossy and deeply savoury.","trivia":"Pork and kimchi are one of Korea's most classic pairings — the pork's fat rounds out the fierce sourness of old kimchi, and the searing step melds them into one flavour before the broth even enters.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi · 1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 1 spring onion · pinch of sugar · pepper","method":["Sear the drained old kimchi hard in hot sesame oil, 3 minutes, until its edges char.","Press the pork slices in and brown with it, 1 minute.","Add the stock, gochujang, soy, garlic and sugar; simmer 15 minutes until red and glossy.","Add the firm tofu in cubes for the last 5 minutes, never stirring it apart.","Serve bubbling hot with steamed rice — alternate stew and plain rice in every bite."]},{"name":"Budget — Tuna Kimchi Jjigae (Bonus: Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"A small tin of tuna replaces the pork — the beloved budget version that turns out to be the pantry match of the century. Skip the tuna and keep the kelp stock for a fully vegan pot.","trivia":"Tinned tuna in kimchi jjigae is not a poverty substitute — it is a beloved Korean classic in its own right. The drained tuna gets browned with the seared kimchi like the pork would, and its mellow fishiness against the sour fermented chilli is one of those pairings that makes no sense until you taste it.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced","to":"1 small tin of tuna, drained (or skip entirely for vegan)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna and press it into the seared kimchi in place of the pork, letting it brown with the pan — the flakes soak up the fermented oil and carry it through the stew. For the vegan version, skip the tuna and use a deep kelp-and-shiitake stock; gochujang carries the body."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Kimchi Stew","icon":"⚡","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Bottled stock and pre-cubed tofu turn the classic into a genuine 15-minute throw-together — the recipe exists for the night the fridge offers nothing but old kimchi, tofu and a busy day.","trivia":"Even fast, the two rules that matter survive: the kimchi gets seared hard before the liquid goes in, and the tofu is added at the end. The stew is as good as the kimchi, and the kimchi is the whole recipe in miniature.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a strong bottled anchovy or vegetable stock, cube the tofu, and skip any browning beyond the kimchi's essential sear. With the kimchi seared and everything else poured together, the pot is on the table in a quarter-hour. The garlic and a pinch of sugar are still non-negotiable."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-gimbap","name":"Gimbap","nameAlt":"Seaweed rice rolls — the Korean picnic and road-trip food","aliases":["gimbap","kimbap","korean sushi roll","seaweed rice roll"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","rolls","picnic","lunchbox","no-cook"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["picnic","lunchbox","road-trip","lunch","everyday"],"mood":"Fresh, cheerful & portable","time":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min (no cooking of the filling beyond what you prep)","health":"Remarkably balanced — rice, lean protein, bright vegetables and sesame oil, with far less sugar or sauce than most lunch options.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil in the rice and filling) · soy (soy sauce in some fillings) · gluten (optional ham/nori)","budget":"★★★ — rice, a few vegetables, and one sheet of nori; it is Korea's classic cheap portable lunch","audience":"The lunchbox and picnic hero — children love it, adults find it satisfying, and it travels better than any sandwich.","season":"Year-round; spring picnics and summer holidays are its natural home","ingredients":"150g cooked short-grain rice (freshly steamed, warm) · 1 sheet nori (seaweed for sushi rolls) · 1 egg, beaten · 40g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks and stir-fried briefly · 40g spinach, wilted and seasoned · 30g pickled radish (danmuji, or a sharp pickle) · 1 slice ham OR 40g bulgogi beef (optional) · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · a few drops of rice vinegar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"GIMBAP IS KOREA'S ROAD-TRIP FOOD — the rolled seaweed-rice snack that travels in the boot (or picnic basket) and gets eaten by hand beside the highway. It is not sushi; it is Korean through and through, seasoned with sesame oil rather than sushi vinegar's sharpness. The two rules that make it: WARM, SEASONED RICE and a ROLL CLOSED TIGHT.\n\n⚖️ THE RICE IS THE WHOLE TRICK — WARM, NOT HOT, AND SEASONED, NOT PLAIN. Cook the rice, and while it is still warm (too hot steams the nori soggy; too cold and the roll falls apart), season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt — and just a few drops of rice vinegar, the lightest echo of near-sushi. Freshly steamed rice is essential: day-old rice is dry and won't stick together in a roll.\n\n⚖️ EVERY FILLING IS PREPPED SEPARATELY, KOREAN-BANCHAN-STYLE. Cut the carrot into fine matchsticks and stir-fry them in a little sesame oil with a pinch of salt until just tender; wilt the spinach, squeeze it DRY (wet filling is the top gimbap killer) and season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt. The pickled radish (danmuji) — if you can find it, it is the bright-yellow punch that defines gimbap — is used raw. The egg: make a thin omelette sheet and slice it into ribbons.\n\nNOW THE ROLL. Lay the nori shiny-side down on a bamboo mat (or, honestly, a clean tea towel works). Spread the warm seasoned rice in an EVEN, THIN layer across the whole sheet, leaving a bare 2cm strip along the far edge — that bare edge is the glue, and a thin rice layer is the difference between a delicate roll and a bready one. Line the fillings in a row just below the centre: the colourful stripes of carrot, spinach, egg, radish and the ham or beef.\n\n⚖️ ROLL IT TIGHT, LIKE A CIGAR, AND SEAL WITH WATER. The signature move of good gimbap is the tight, even roll — pull the mat's near edge up and over, press the filling into a cylinder, and roll forward firmly, compressing as you go. Dip your finger in water, run it along the bare nori edge, and seal the seam. Rest the sealed roll seam-down for a minute.\n\nSLICE with a very sharp, slightly wet knife — sawing drags the rice out. Brush the finished roll lightly with sesame oil and scatter toasted sesame seeds over it if you want the classic glistening finish. Slice into bite-size rounds and pack them for the journey.\n\nREGIONAL NOTE FOR THE GLOBAL READER: danmuji (the yellow pickled radish) is gimbap's secret weapon and almost defines the taste — it is a bright, sweet-tangy pickle sold at Asian grocers. A sharp pickled cucumber or a good chow-chow-style pickle stands in honestly if you cannot find it.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 68g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Gimbap is made for the road but is best eaten the same day — the nori softens and the rice dries with time. Keeps a day wrapped tightly in plastic, or up to 2 days in the fridge if the roll is very tight. To revive, brush with sesame oil and wrap in a damp cloth for an hour — the classic picnic trick.","chefNotes":"The two make-or-break rules: warm, sesame-oiled rice (never dry day-old) and a tight roll. Wet filling is the silent killer — squeeze every filling dry. The bare nori edge sealed with water, and slicing with a wet knife, are the two professional moves that turn a roll into gimbap.","pairsWith":"A can of cold cola or barley tea · Kimchi on the side · A crisp soup (many Koreans eat gimbap with a side of mandu-guk or a simple egg drop soup)","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Gimbap evolved from Japanese nori-maki during the Japanese occupation, but it became entirely its own: Korean gimbap uses sesame-oil-seasoned rice (not vinegar-heavy sushi rice), a wider range of cooked vegetables, and is eaten as everyday portable food rather than ceremony. The word gimbap means simply 'seaweed-rice', gim being the Korean word for the nori sheet. It is so tied to travel that Koreans call it 'the official food of the Korean road trip' — sold in bulk at rest stops and gas-station convenience stores, picked up on the way to both the beach and the mountains. The yellow danmuji pickle inside is so essential that any Korean grocery sells tubs of it specifically for gimbap making, and the number of gimbap franchises in Korea runs into the thousands.","howThisFeels":"Unrolling the mat to reveal a perfect glossy cylinder, slicing it into neat rounds with a wet knife — and the small triumph of a bite that is rice, egg, sesame and pickle all at once, eaten warm by the side of the road or cool from the lunchbox.","leftovers":["Fried gimbap (savoury leftover move): slice the day-old rolls thick and pan-fry them in a little oil until golden — the Korean way to make yesterday's gimbap exciting again.","Wrap leftover gimbap in a damp cloth for an hour to revive the nori before eating.","Dice it into a rice bowl with a fried egg for gimbap-style fried rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bulgogi Gimbap","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seasoned rice, carrot, spinach, egg ribbon, pickled radish and a strip of the marinated beef (or ham). The full gimbap experience.","trivia":"Bulgogi gimbap is the upmarket home version — strips of the pear-marinated beef add a savoury-sweet depth that plain ham cannot match. In Korea the convenience-store version is the daily standard; the home version with good beef is for guests.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g warm short-grain rice · 1 sheet nori · 1 egg · 40g carrot · 40g spinach · 30g pickled radish (danmuji) · 1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · rice vinegar · sesame seeds","method":["Season the warm rice with sesame oil, salt and a few drops of vinegar.","Prep the fillings separately: stir-fried carrot, wilted-and-squeezed spinach, egg ribbon (thin omelette sheet sliced), pickled radish.","Spread rice in a thin even layer on the nori, leaving a 2cm bare edge.","Line the colourful fillings just below centre, with the beef or ham as the anchor.","Roll TIGHT with a mat, seal the bare edge with water, rest seam-down.","Slice with a wet knife; brush with sesame oil and scatter sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Tuna Mayo Gimbap","icon":"💰","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Tinned tuna mixed with a little mayo stands in for the pricier beef — the beloved budget filling that the whole country orders from convenience stores.","trivia":"Tuna-mayo gimbap is a genuine Korean favourite, not a compromise one: the creamy tuna against the sesame rice and crunchy pickle is why it is one of the best-selling convenience-store gimbaps in Korea.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef","to":"80g tinned tuna, drained, mixed with a little mayo and a pinch of sesame"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna well, mix with a spoonful of mayo, a few drops of sesame oil and a pinch of salt. Use it exactly where the beef would sit. It rolls tighter with a softer filling, so press gently as you roll."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian (Yachae) Gimbap","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"Every colour the roll can carry — carrot, spinach, egg-free, pickled radish, cucumber — and honestly the crispest, freshest version of all.","trivia":"Vegetable gimbap (yachae gimbap) is a standard make in Korean households, and the pickled radish is what carries its bright tang. For a fully vegan roll, simply skip the egg and use seasoned tofu or just more vegetables — the roll loses nothing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g extra vegetables (cucumber, seasoned tofu) in place of the protein and egg"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add cucumber matchsticks and a strip of seasoned or smoked tofu for the savoury anchor. Squeeze the cucumber dry so the roll stays crisp. The sesame-oiled rice and the pickle do the rest — this is the freshest, most colourful gimbap of the set."}]}}]},
];
